</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.D.3 </span>Translator Module Ablation</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We evaluate whether the non-linear translator can be replaced by a simpler projection.
Replacing the translator with a linear projection under the same input normalization reduces performance from <math id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">59.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="49.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">49.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">49.3</annotation></semantics></math> mAP<math id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{50\text{-}95}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">50</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">95</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{50\text{-}95}</annotation></semantics></math> on PerSeg and from <math id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">36.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36.3</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">29.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29.4</annotation></semantics></math> on POD 1-shot.
On held-out regions, the linear projection also yields a higher MSE (<math id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.24</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.09" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.09</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.09</annotation></semantics></math>) and a much weaker teacher-student distance correlation (<math id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.300</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="Pt0.A4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.755" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.755</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.755</annotation></semantics></math>).
This confirms that the translator is not merely a dimensionality adapter, but bridges a non-linear semantic gap between detector features and VLM embeddings.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.D.4 </span>Dimensionality Reduction</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#Pt0.A5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 0.E.2 MOCHA Pseudocode ‣ Appendix 0.E Implementation Details ‣ MOCHA: Multi-modal Objects-aware Cross-arcHitecture Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows the variance profile of teacher embeddings, with a fitted decay curve of the form <math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\simeq\frac{a}{(x+1)^{b}}+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>b</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\simeq\frac{a}{(x+1)^{b}}+c</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming the typical power-law structure that motivates dimensionality reduction.
The standard deviation of each channel activation in the distillation dataset is estimated and used to rescale the channel <math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> as: <math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{c}\simeq\frac{18}{(c+1)^{0.47}}-0.26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>18</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0.47</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>0.26</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{c}\simeq\frac{18}{(c+1)^{0.47}}-0.26</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. 3 in the main document).
<a href="#Pt0.A5.T12" title="In 0.E.2 MOCHA Pseudocode ‣ Appendix 0.E Implementation Details ‣ MOCHA: Multi-modal Objects-aware Cross-arcHitecture Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tables</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, <a href="#Pt0.A5.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 0.E.2 MOCHA Pseudocode ‣ Appendix 0.E Implementation Details ‣ MOCHA: Multi-modal Objects-aware Cross-arcHitecture Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and <a href="#Pt0.A5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ 0.E.2 MOCHA Pseudocode ‣ Appendix 0.E Implementation Details ‣ MOCHA: Multi-modal Objects-aware Cross-arcHitecture Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> report extensive results across all datasets and protocols for different PCA settings under three configurations:</p>
<ul id="Pt0.A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Pt0.A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Pt0.A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PCA on <math id="Pt0.A4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in Personal Dataset (Tab. <a href="#Pt0.A5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ 0.E.2 MOCHA Pseudocode ‣ Appendix 0.E Implementation Details ‣ MOCHA: Multi-modal Objects-aware Cross-arcHitecture Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), where PCA is applied on teacher embeddings of each personal dataset (ideal case);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Pt0.A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Pt0.A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PCA on <math id="Pt0.A4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in Distillation Dataset (Tab. <a href="#Pt0.A5.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ 0.E.2 MOCHA Pseudocode ‣ Appendix 0.E Implementation Details ‣ MOCHA: Multi-modal Objects-aware Cross-arcHitecture Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), where PCA is applied on teacher embeddings of distillation dataset;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Pt0.A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Pt0.A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PCA on <math id="Pt0.A4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in Distillation Dataset (Tab. <a href="#Pt0.A5.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 0.E.2 MOCHA Pseudocode ‣ Appendix 0.E Implementation Details ‣ MOCHA: Multi-modal Objects-aware Cross-arcHitecture Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), where the channel normalization of Fig. <a href="#Pt0.A5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 0.E.2 MOCHA Pseudocode ‣ Appendix 0.E Implementation Details ‣ MOCHA: Multi-modal Objects-aware Cross-arcHitecture Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> is applied prior to PCA.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Across all experiments, we observe that moderate compression (<span id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, retaining <math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions) achieves competitive performance, with minimal loss relative to full-dimensional embeddings, while substantially reducing memory and compute costs. As expected, aggressive compression (<span id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, <math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions) leads to consistent drops across datasets, particularly on POD and iCubWorld.
Overall, this analysis confirms that PCA acts as an effective mechanism to control embedding size while preserving sufficient discriminative information for distillation. The results validate the robustness of our approach to dimensionality reduction, supporting the choice of PCA dimension <math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}=512</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To verify that PCA does not collapse the fused representation into a single modality, we analyze the relative contribution of each modality to the retained 512 components.
We compute this contribution by summing squared PCA loadings associated with the dimensions of <math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{V,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{V,i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The analysis assigns 31.7% of the contribution to <math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">h</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and 68.3% to <math id="Pt0.A4.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{V,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">V</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{V,i}</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming that the fused target preserves both high-level semantic and fine-grained visual information.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.D.5 </span>Relational Loss</h3>

<div id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">To further clarify the role of the relational embedding loss, we report in Tab. </span><a href="#Pt0.A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ 0.D.5 Relational Loss ‣ Appendix 0.D Additional Ablation Studies ‣ MOCHA: Multi-modal Objects-aware Cross-arcHitecture Alignment" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a><span id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> an additional ablation that explores lightweight approximations based on Top-</span><math id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or Rand-</span><math id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> pair sampling, as well as alternative normalization choices.
While sampling can reduce the cost of computing pairwise relations, it consistently underperforms the full formulation, and the gap remains non-negligible on both PerSeg and POD.
In particular, increasing </span><math id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> yields only marginal accuracy gains, suggesting that partial relational views are insufficient to faithfully preserve the global geometry of the teacher space.
Likewise, switching from the proposed </span><math id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{dist}/\sqrt{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">dist</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msqrt style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">d</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{dist}/\sqrt{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> scaling to </span><math id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-normalization provides limited benefits and does not close the performance gap.
Overall, these results support our design choice: leveraging all pairs delivers the most reliable improvements, whereas sampling mainly trades accuracy for modest efficiency gains in our setting.
</span><span id="Pt0.A4.SS5.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
